.PP
Characters are now escaped using single quote symbols (\fR'\fP) instead of square brackets\&. \fRformat\fP now throws RangeError if it encounters an unescaped latin character that isn't a valid formatting token\&.

.PP
To use \fRYY\fP and \fRYYYY\fP tokens that represent week-numbering years, you should set \fRuseAdditionalWeekYearTokens\fP option:

.PP
.PP
.nf
format(Date\&.now(), "YY", { useAdditionalWeekYearTokens: true });
//=> '86'
.fi
.PP

.PP
To use \fRD\fP and \fRDD\fP tokens which represent days of the year, set \fRuseAdditionalDayOfYearTokens\fP option:

.PP
.PP
.nf
format(Date\&.now(), "D", { useAdditionalDayOfYearTokens: true });
//=> '364'
.fi
.PP

.PP
.IP "\(bu" 2
\fBBREAKING\fP: function submodules now use camelCase naming schema:

.PP
.PP
.nf
// Before v2\&.0\&.0
import differenceInCalendarISOYears from "date\-fns/difference_in_calendar_iso_years";

// v2\&.0\&.0 onward
import differenceInCalendarISOYears from "date\-fns/differenceInCalendarISOYears";
.fi
.PP

.IP "\(bu" 2
\fBBREAKING\fP: min and max functions now accept an array of dates rather than spread arguments\&.

.PP
.PP
.nf
// Before v2\&.0\&.0
var date1 = new Date(1989, 6 /* Jul */, 10);
var date2 = new Date(1987, 1 /* Feb */, 11);

var minDate = min(date1, date2);
var maxDate = max(date1, date2);

// v2\&.0\&.0 onward:
var dates = [
  new Date(1989, 6 /* Jul */, 10),
  new Date(1987, 1 /* Feb */, 11),
];

var minDate = min(dates);
var maxDate = max(dates);
.fi
.PP

.IP "\(bu" 2
\fBBREAKING\fP: make the second argument of \fRformat\fP required for the sake of explicitness\&.

.PP
.PP
.nf
// Before v2\&.0\&.0
format(new Date(2016, 0, 1));

// v2\&.0\&.0 onward
format(new Date(2016, 0, 1), "yyyy\-MM\-dd'T'HH:mm:ss\&.SSSxxx");
.fi
.PP

.IP "\(bu" 2
\fBBREAKING\fP renamed ISO week-numbering year helpers:
.IP "  \(bu" 4
\fRaddISOYears\fP → \fRaddISOWeekYears\fP
.IP "  \(bu" 4
\fRdifferenceInCalendarISOYears\fP → \fRdifferenceInCalendarISOWeekYears\fP
.IP "  \(bu" 4
\fRdifferenceInISOYears\fP → \fRdifferenceInISOWeekYears\fP
.IP "  \(bu" 4
\fRendOfISOYear\fP → \fRendOfISOWeekYear\fP
.IP "  \(bu" 4
\fRgetISOYear\fP → \fRgetISOWeekYear\fP
.IP "  \(bu" 4
\fRisSameISOYear\fP → \fRisSameISOWeekYear\fP
.IP "  \(bu" 4
\fRlastDayOfISOYear\fP → \fRlastDayOfISOWeekYear\fP
.IP "  \(bu" 4
\fRsetISOYear\fP → \fRsetISOWeekYear\fP
.IP "  \(bu" 4
\fRsubISOYears\fP → \fRsubISOWeekYears\fP
.PP

.PP
i\&.e\&. "ISO year" renamed to "ISO week year", which is short for \fRISO week-numbering year\fP\&. It makes them consistent with locale-dependent week-numbering year helpers, e\&.g\&., \fRstartOfWeekYear\fP\&.
.IP "\(bu" 2
\fBBREAKING\fP: functions renamed:
.IP "  \(bu" 4
\fRareRangesOverlapping\fP → \fRareIntervalsOverlapping\fP
.IP "  \(bu" 4
\fReachDay\fP → \fReachDayOfInterval\fP
.IP "  \(bu" 4
\fRgetOverlappingDaysInRanges\fP → \fRgetOverlappingDaysInIntervals\fP
.IP "  \(bu" 4
\fRisWithinRange\fP → \fRisWithinInterval\fP
.PP

.PP
This change was made to mirror the use of the word "interval" in standard ISO 8601:2004 terminology:

.PP
.PP
.nf
2\&.1\&.3
time interval
part of the time axis limited by two instants
.fi
.PP

.PP
Also these functions now accept an object with \fRstart\fP and \fRend\fP properties instead of two arguments as an interval\&. All these functions throw \fRRangeError\fP if the start of the interval is after its end or if any date in the interval is \fRInvalid Date\fP\&.

.PP
.PP
.nf
// Before v2\&.0\&.0

areRangesOverlapping(
  new Date(2014, 0, 10),
  new Date(2014, 0, 20),
  new Date(2014, 0, 17),
  new Date(2014, 0, 21),
);

eachDay(new Date(2014, 0, 10), new Date(2014, 0, 20));

getOverlappingDaysInRanges(
  new Date(2014, 0, 10),
  new Date(2014, 0, 20),
  new Date(2014, 0, 17),
  new Date(2014, 0, 21),
);

isWithinRange(
  new Date(2014, 0, 3),
  new Date(2014, 0, 1),
  new Date(2014, 0, 7),
);

// v2\&.0\&.0 onward

areIntervalsOverlapping(
  { start: new Date(2014, 0, 10), end: new Date(2014, 0, 20) },
  { start: new Date(2014, 0, 17), end: new Date(2014, 0, 21) },
);

eachDayOfInterval({
  start: new Date(2014, 0, 10),
  end: new Date(2014, 0, 20),
});

getOverlappingDaysInIntervals(
  { start: new Date(2014, 0, 10), end: new Date(2014, 0, 20) },
  { start: new Date(2014, 0, 17), end: new Date(2014, 0, 21) },
);

isWithinInterval(new Date(2014, 0, 3), {
  start: new Date(2014, 0, 1),
  end: new Date(2014, 0, 7),
});
.fi
.PP

.IP "\(bu" 2
\fBBREAKING\fP: functions renamed:
.IP "  \(bu" 4
\fRdistanceInWords\fP → \fRformatDistance\fP
.IP "  \(bu" 4
\fRdistanceInWordsStrict\fP → \fRformatDistanceStrict\fP
.IP "  \(bu" 4
\fRdistanceInWordsToNow\fP → \fRformatDistanceToNow\fP
.PP

.PP
to make them consistent with \fRformat\fP and \fRformatRelative\fP\&.
.IP "\(bu" 2
\fBBREAKING\fP: The order of arguments of \fRdistanceInWords\fP and \fRdistanceInWordsStrict\fP is swapped to make them consistent with \fRdifferenceIn\&.\&.\&.\fP functions\&.

.PP
.PP
.nf
// Before v2\&.0\&.0

distanceInWords(
  new Date(1986, 3, 4, 10, 32, 0),
  new Date(1986, 3, 4, 11, 32, 0),
  { addSuffix: true },
); //=> 'in about 1 hour'

// v2\&.0\&.0 onward

formatDistance(
  new Date(1986, 3, 4, 11, 32, 0),
  new Date(1986, 3, 4, 10, 32, 0),
  { addSuffix: true },
); //=> 'in about 1 hour'
.fi
.PP

.IP "\(bu" 2
\fBBREAKING\fP: \fRpartialMethod\fP option in \fRformatDistanceStrict\fP is renamed to \fRroundingMethod\fP\&.

.PP
.PP
.nf
// Before v2\&.0\&.0

distanceInWordsStrict(
  new Date(1986, 3, 4, 10, 32, 0),
  new Date(1986, 3, 4, 10, 33, 1),
  { partialMethod: "ceil" },
); //=> '2 minutes'

// v2\&.0\&.0 onward

formatDistanceStrict(
  new Date(1986, 3, 4, 10, 33, 1),
  new Date(1986, 3, 4, 10, 32, 0),
  { roundingMethod: "ceil" },
); //=> '2 minutes'
.fi
.PP

.IP "\(bu" 2
\fBBREAKING\fP: in \fRformatDistanceStrict\fP, if \fRroundingMethod\fP is not specified, it now defaults to \fRround\fP instead of \fRfloor\fP\&.
.IP "\(bu" 2
\fBBREAKING\fP: \fRunit\fP option in \fRformatDistanceStrict\fP now accepts one of the strings: 'second', 'minute', 'hour', 'day', 'month' or 'year' instead of 's', 'm', 'h', 'd', 'M' or 'Y'

.PP
.PP
.nf
// Before v2\&.0\&.0

distanceInWordsStrict(
  new Date(1986, 3, 4, 10, 32, 0),
  new Date(1986, 3, 4, 10, 33, 1),
  { unit: "m" },
);

// v2\&.0\&.0 onward

formatDistanceStrict(
  new Date(1986, 3, 4, 10, 33, 1),
  new Date(1986, 3, 4, 10, 32, 0),
  { unit: "minute" },
);
.fi
.PP

.IP "\(bu" 2
\fBBREAKING\fP: \fRparse\fP that previously used to convert strings and numbers to dates now parses only strings in an arbitrary format specified as an argument\&. Use \fRtoDate\fP to coerce numbers and \fRparseISO\fP to parse ISO 8601 strings\&.

.PP
.PP
.nf
// Before v2\&.0\&.0
parse("2016\-01\-01");
parse(1547005581366);
parse(new Date()); // Clone the date

// v2\&.0\&.0 onward
parse("2016\-01\-01", "yyyy\-MM\-dd", new Date());
parseISO("2016\-01\-01");
toDate(1547005581366);
toDate(new Date()); // Clone the date
.fi
.PP

.IP "\(bu" 2
\fBBREAKING\fP: \fRtoDate\fP (previously \fRparse\fP) now doesn't accept string arguments but only numbers and dates\&. \fRtoDate\fP called with an invalid argument will return \fRInvalid Date\fP\&.
.IP "\(bu" 2
\fBBREAKING\fP: new locale format\&. See \fRdocs/Locale\fP\&. Locales renamed:
.IP "  \(bu" 4
\fRen\fP → \fRen-US\fP
.IP "  \(bu" 4
\fRzh_cn\fP → \fRzh-CN\fP
.IP "  \(bu" 4
\fRzh_tw\fP → \fRzh-TW\fP
.PP

.PP
.PP
.nf
// Before v2\&.0\&.0
import locale from "date\-fns/locale/zh_cn";

// v2\&.0\&.0 onward
import locale from "date\-fns/locale/zh\-CN";
.fi
.PP

.IP "\(bu" 2
\fBBREAKING\fP: now \fRclosestTo\fP and \fRclosestIndexTo\fP don't throw an exception when the second argument is not an array, and return Invalid Date instead\&.
.IP "\(bu" 2
\fBBREAKING\fP: now \fRisValid\fP doesn't throw an exception if the first argument is not an instance of Date\&. Instead, argument is converted beforehand using \fRtoDate\fP\&.

.PP
Examples:
.PP

.PP
\fRisValid\fP argument  Before v2\&.0\&.0  v2\&.0\&.0 onward  \fRnew Date()\fP  \fRtrue\fP  \fRtrue\fP  \fRnew Date('2016-01-01')\fP  \fRtrue\fP  \fRtrue\fP  \fRnew Date('')\fP  \fRfalse\fP  \fRfalse\fP  \fRnew Date(1488370835081)\fP  \fRtrue\fP  \fRtrue\fP  \fRnew Date(NaN)\fP  \fRfalse\fP  \fRfalse\fP  \fR'2016-01-01'\fP  \fRTypeError\fP  \fRfalse\fP  \fR''\fP  \fRTypeError\fP  \fRfalse\fP  \fR1488370835081\fP  \fRTypeError\fP  \fRtrue\fP  \fRNaN\fP  \fRTypeError\fP  \fRfalse\fP  

.PP
We introduce this change to make \fIdate-fns\fP consistent with ECMAScript behavior that try to coerce arguments to the expected type (which is also the case with other \fIdate-fns\fP functions)\&.

.PP
.IP "\(bu" 2
\fBBREAKING\fP: functions now throw \fRRangeError\fP if optional values passed to \fRoptions\fP are not \fRundefined\fP or have expected values\&. This change is introduced for consistency with ECMAScript standard library which does the same\&.
.IP "\(bu" 2
\fBBREAKING\fP: \fRformat\fP, \fRformatDistance\fP (previously \fRdistanceInWords\fP) and \fRformatDistanceStrict\fP (previously \fRdistanceInWordsStrict\fP) now throw \fRRangeError\fP if one of the passed arguments is invalid\&. It reflects behavior of \fRtoISOString\fP and Intl API\&. See \fR#1032\fP\&.
.IP "\(bu" 2
\fBBREAKING\fP: all functions now implicitly convert arguments by following rules:
.PP

.PP
date  number  string  boolean  0  new Date(0)  0  '0'  false  '0'  Invalid Date  0  '0'  false  1  new Date(1)  1  '1'  true  '1'  Invalid Date  1  '1'  true  true  Invalid Date  NaN  'true'  true  false  Invalid Date  NaN  'false'  false  null  Invalid Date  NaN  'null'  false  undefined  Invalid Date  NaN  'undefined'  false  NaN  Invalid Date  NaN  'NaN'  false  

.PP
Notes:

.PP
.IP "\(bu" 2
as before, arguments expected to be \fRDate\fP are converted to \fRDate\fP using \fIdate-fns'\fP \fRtoDate\fP function;
.IP "\(bu" 2
arguments expected to be numbers are converted to integer numbers using our custom \fRtoInteger\fP implementation (see \fR#765\fP);
.IP "\(bu" 2
arguments expected to be strings are converted to strings using JavaScript's \fRString\fP function;
.IP "\(bu" 2
arguments expected to be booleans are converted to boolean using JavaScript's \fRBoolean\fP function\&.
.PP

.PP
\fRnull\fP and \fRundefined\fP passed to optional arguments (i\&.e\&. properties of \fRoptions\fP argument) are ignored as if no argument was passed\&.

.PP
If any resulting argument is invalid (i\&.e\&. \fRNaN\fP for numbers and \fRInvalid Date\fP for dates), an invalid value will be returned:

.PP
.IP "\(bu" 2
\fRfalse\fP for functions that return booleans (expect \fRisValid\fP);
.IP "\(bu" 2
\fRInvalid Date\fP for functions that return dates;
.IP "\(bu" 2
and \fRNaN\fP for functions that return numbers\&.
.PP

.PP
See tests and PRs \fR#460\fP and \fR#765\fP for exact behavior\&.

.PP
.IP "\(bu" 2
\fBBREAKING\fP: all functions now check if the passed number of arguments is less than the number of required arguments and will throw \fRTypeError\fP exception if so\&.
.IP "\(bu" 2
\fBBREAKING\fP: all functions that accept numbers as arguments, now coerce values using \fRNumber()\fP and also round off decimals\&. Positive decimals are rounded using \fRMath\&.floor\fP, decimals less than zero are rounded using \fRMath\&.ceil\fP\&.
.IP "\(bu" 2
\fBBREAKING\fP: The Bower & UMD/CDN package versions are no longer supported\&.
.IP "\(bu" 2
\fBBREAKING\fP: \fRnull\fP now is not a valid date\&. \fRisValid(null)\fP returns \fRfalse\fP; \fRtoDate(null)\fP returns an invalid date\&. Since \fRtoDate\fP is used internally by all the functions, operations over \fRnull\fP will also return an invalid date\&. \fRSee #537\fP for the reasoning\&.
.IP "\(bu" 2
\fRtoDate\fP (previously \fRparse\fP) and \fRisValid\fP functions now accept \fRany\fP type as the first argument\&.
.IP "\(bu" 2
\fRExclude \fRdocs\&.json\fP from the npm package\fP\&. Kudos to \fR@hawkrives\fP\&.
.PP
.SS "Added"
.IP "\(bu" 2
FP functions like those in \fRlodash\fP, that support \fRcurrying\fP, and, as a consequence, functional-style \fRfunction composing\fP\&.

.PP
Functions with options (\fRformat\fP, \fRparse\fP, etc\&.) have two FP counterparts: one that has the options object as its first argument and one that hasn't\&. The name of the former has \fRWithOptions\fP added to the end of its name\&.

.PP
In FP functions, the order of arguments is reversed\&.

.PP
See \fRFP Guide\fP for more information\&.

.PP
.PP
.nf
import addYears from "date\-fns/fp/addYears";
import formatWithOptions from "date\-fns/fp/formatWithOptions";
import eo from "date\-fns/locale/eo";

// If FP function has not received enough arguments, it returns another function
const addFiveYears = addYears(5);

// Several arguments can be curried at once
const dateToString = formatWithOptions({ locale: eo }, "d MMMM yyyy");

const dates = [
  new Date(2017, 0 /* Jan */, 1),
  new Date(2017, 1 /* Feb */, 11),
  new Date(2017, 6 /* Jul */, 2),
];

const formattedDates = dates\&.map((date) => dateToString(addFiveYears(date)));
//=> ['1 januaro 2022', '11 februaro 2022', '2 julio 2022']
.fi
.PP

.IP "\(bu" 2
Added support for \fRECMAScript Modules\fP\&.

.PP
It allows usage with bundlers that support tree-shaking, like \fRrollup\&.js\fP and \fRwebpack\fP:

.PP
.PP
.nf
// Without tree\-shaking:
import format from "date\-fns/format";
import parse from "date\-fns/parse";

// With tree\-shaking:
import { format, parse } from "date\-fns";
.fi
.PP

.PP
Also, ESM functions provide default export, they can be used with TypeScript to import functions in more idiomatic way:

.PP
.PP
.nf
// Before
import * as format from "date\-fns/format";

// Now
import format from "date\-fns/format";
.fi
.PP

